    "EditNoteBetween": "Another note lies between the two, so they cannot merge. Only two notes in a row make a chord.",
    "EditKeys": "Selecting\nClick|Selects one note. A chord's two notes are selected together.\nCtrl+click|Adds the note to the selection or takes it out.\nShift+click|Selects everything from the last selected note to this one.\nDrag|Selects the notes inside the box. Hold Ctrl or Shift to add to the selection.\nCtrl+A|Selects every note.\nEsc|Clears the selection.\nw / s|Moves the selection to the next (up) or previous (down) note; the view follows.\nLanes\na / d|Moves the selected notes one lane left or right. Nothing moves if any of them would hit a wall.\n1 to 8|Slides the whole selection until it reaches that lane, keeping its shape.\nCtrl+F|Flips the selection left to right within the lanes it spans (on lanes 5 and 6, 5 and 6 swap). With an odd number of lanes, the middle lane stays.\nChords\nCtrl+R|Chooses for you: splits when only chords are selected, merges every possible pair when only single notes are. Does nothing when both are selected.\nCtrl+M|Merges every pair of selected single notes that can be one chord (within 40ms, no note between them, lanes side by side). Chords stay.\nCtrl+U|Splits every selected chord. Single notes stay.\nView\nWheel|Rolling up moves toward later notes.\nCtrl+wheel|Zooms in and out around the pointer.\nShift+wheel|Moves ten times faster.\nSaving and closing\nCtrl+Z|Undoes. Ctrl+Shift+Z redoes.\nCtrl+S|Opens the window that picks a slot to save into.\nCtrl+Shift+S|Asks to overwrite the slot being edited, then saves (an original song opens the slot picker).\nCtrl+X|Closes; asks first when there are unsaved changes.\nCtrl+T|Opens the terminal, the same shortcut as in the main window.\nKeys work only while the editor window has the focus.",
