    "ReleaseNotes.1.0.3": "· This build includes the timing correction for three-planet sections, as in THE END OF HATSUNE MIKU. Sync maps once, then reload the song.\n· The app version and applied date now identify this build.\n· When link auto cannot match a song to the game, the main screen says so, and asks for a map sync when an older cache is why.",
    "ReleaseNotes.1.0.4": "· Fewer awkward patterns. A 2K stretch that suddenly speeds up goes to 4K at once instead of bringing a lane straight back, the single after a chord sits beside it (1-2 3), a single before a trill takes 3 2-1 first, and a roll that reaches the edge rolls back.\n· The tile count can now come from both gaps (the shorter one).\n· Experimental, off by default: enhanced chord and trill rules keep a chord in place where one to three singles come between chords.\n· Restarting the pattern on a run no longer brings the lane just played straight back.\n· Console link inspect (for measuring only): with No Fail on, it presses the visualizer's notes into the game to check the timing. Install the linker again with install linker.",
    "ReleaseNotes.1.0.5": "· link inspect is now link -inspect start in the console, and link -inspect stop ends it.\n· Console load link, info link, id link and title link: the song the game is playing (with the link on).\n· link inspect no longer stops at the first note while the game is not yet in front, and shows a banner in the middle of the notes while it runs.\n· Loading the same song or another version again shows the message as just now, and a slot change or a settings change that moves the tiles says so, as in 'The tiles changed: Original → Edit 1'.\n· The console follows the app's language: with the language set to Korean, command help, results and questions are all in Korean (command and option names and song titles stay as they are).\n· version now shows the version at once; version -check shows the applied date, the build time and whether it is the latest.",
    "ReleaseNotes.1.0.6": "· inspect is now a command: inspect start, inspect stop and inspect result. -runs:3 shows the last three runs; -runs:* shows all.\n· inspect -flow start prints keys, notes, tiles and timing as they happen. The console takes no commands until the run ends.\n· During countdown, inspect taps the eight lane keys, Space and Enter before the first note. Waiting for the game no longer skips the first tile.\n· Console log keeps link, game and note events in memory; report -log create <title> saves them into a note.\n· link stop turns link auto off too. load link works with link auto off.\n· update offers to rebuild when the running app is older than the files in its folder.",
    "ReleaseNotes.1.0.8": "· Countdown warm-up now waits for the game window and keeps delayed taps separate. It finishes 100 ms before the first note.\n· Inspection results retain the run settings, original map tiles and same-key repeat intervals to help investigate missing inputs. App timing and observed game samples are labeled separately.\n· Restarting the game during inspection no longer produces negative judgment totals.",
    "ReleaseNotes.1.0.7": "· Restarting inspect now restores notes when the game rewinds and resumes the app clock if it was left paused.\n· A new inspection no longer ends immediately against the previous play position.\n· Countdown warm-up also works when restarting at a checkpoint.",
    "ReleaseNotes.1.0.2": "· The game link holds up better: it connects again after the game restarts, and the notes wait where they are while the game is paused, stands still, plays another level or a speed other than 100%, or goes quiet.\n· The linker in the game no longer stops over a passing error in the game, and no longer searches every frame when the key viewer mod is missing. Install the new one with install linker in the console, then restart the game.\n· Closing the editor and opening it again goes back to where you were in that song (until the app closes).\n· Saving an edit and loading it again keeps the practice section.\n· The linker in the game could send the song position a frame late; notes could fall behind in songs with heavy effects.\n· Ctrl+T opens the console with the game or any window of this app in front (the song list and help included), also while typing in Hangul. The editor's keys work in Hangul mode too.\n· The first and last notes keep their colors after they are pressed. With a start key that plays the first note, the start color was never seen.\n· The song-end band fits the buttons it holds, without the empty room left below when there is no last-checkpoint button. The first button stays where it was.\n· With the game followed, the notes no longer run on between a game over and the restart, and the notes of the new attempt are no longer at risk of staying hidden.\n· Songs with a pause whose planet correction runs Forward (such as 柿崎ユウタ - 月が綺麗ねと言われたい！) now load: the direction only turns the planet as drawn, not the timing. The parser changed, so run a map sync once.\n· Maps with a doubled comma in their settings (such as Plum - PLUM MEGAMIX 2) now load; all 6545 tiles match the times the game itself works out. Run a map sync once.\n· With the game followed, the notes no longer run on while a level waits for its first key, including the screen after a game over.\n· Trills read better: fewer runs that drop a lane (4 3-2 1 3 2-1), bring a lane straight back (3 4 3) or turn back (2 4-3). A trill uses the center pair only to keep the run going (setting: Trill center pair last).\n· The note after a long note never takes its lane. The long note width applies to the held part only, so head and tail are note wide, like an I.\n· Magic circles have a new setting, Chords in magic circles.\n· link auto recognizes edits and picks up again after moving to another song. While it is on, keys such as Enter no longer move the notes.\n· Sections where a third planet joins, as in THE END OF HATSUNE MIKU, now keep the game's timing (checked against the game's own tile times). Sync the maps once.\n· With link auto on, the song you move to in the game is loaded by itself.",
    "ReleaseNotes.1.0.1": "· Note placement reads better: the same key no longer comes twice in a row where it can be avoided (3 3 2 3 2, 3-4 3-4), and a single between chords sits right beside them (2 3-4 2 3-4).\n· Trills follow the pattern's direction: on a 4-3-2-1 pattern they read 4-3 and 2-1, two trills in a row take two pairs, and the note after a trill sits right beside it (3 after 1-2).\n· The first and last notes are a deep yellow by default. Colors you already changed stay as they are.\n· Hide on the song details now folds away the controls and the dark band too, leaving only a Show button.\n· The song box always takes typing; the search switch is gone from the settings.\n· The song editor keeps its tools beside the sheet: every shortcut has a button, and the zoom shows in percent with a reset.\n· The console has pin, unpin and go: pin the tiles you practise and pick them with Tab after go. An original and its edits share their pins.\n· The linker (the game link mod) comes with the app, so the console's install linker puts it in place on its own.",
    "ReleaseNotes.1.0.0": "· The first full release.\n· The app has its own icon.\n· The first note of each play and the song's last note have their own colors, shown over the checkpoint color. Change them in Settings > Colors.\n· A second after a song ends, it offers to play again. The reset key (Enter by default) closes the offer and goes back to the last checkpoint, or to the beginning when there is none or checkpoints are not automatic.\n· A song that starts on a chord no longer shows one half of it pressed as play starts.\n· Songs and settings are kept in one place on this PC, so the app shows the same song list and settings wherever it runs from.",
