  // The console speaks the app's language too. Command, option and keyword names stay as they are typed.
  "console": {
    "Summary.cmd": "Show the commands.",
    "Help.cmd": "Show every command, grouped by what it does.",
    "Summary.help": "Explain a command.",
    "Help.help": "Explain a command, with its options and keywords. With nothing, every command.",
    "Summary.option": "Show the options.",
    "Help.option": "With nothing, every option and what it does; with a command, that command's options.",
    "Summary.keyword": "Show the keywords.",
    "Help.keyword": "With nothing, every keyword and the commands that take it; with a command, that command's keywords.",
    "Summary.version": "Show the app version.",
    "Help.version": "Show the app version at once. -check adds the date it was applied and when this build was made, and asks the repository whether it is the latest version, which takes a moment. -number prints the number alone.",
    "Summary.fps": "Show how smoothly it draws.",
    "Help.fps": "Show the frame intervals of the last few seconds, how long the app itself took in them, the garbage collections meanwhile and the render tier. -restart starts a new measurement after printing.",
    "Summary.link": "Show or drive the game link.",
    "Help.link": "With nothing, show what the read-only sync mod is sending: how often readings arrive, how much the delay varies, the game's state, speed and level, and the state changes so far. start opens the pipe and stop closes it, which ends link auto too; tiles compares the game's own tile times with the loaded song; follow puts the notes on the game's clock and unfollow takes them back; again starts a new measurement. Nothing is sent to the game and nothing is saved; inspect is the one command that presses keys into it, for measuring.",
    "Summary.install": "Install the game link mod, or put a part back.",
    "Help.install": "install linker writes the read-only link mod into the game's own Mods folder, which is the one place outside this app's folders it ever writes, and only when Unity Mod Manager is there. It asks first, writes the mod's two files and nothing else, and leaves other mods alone. A mod already there with the same version is left as it is. -reinstall writes a part again when scannow finds it wrong: source puts this version's files back, app builds it again, linker writes this version's mod again, maps builds the custom maps again, and all is this version in full. Your edits and data are your own and are never reinstalled. Name several with commas, as in install -reinstall app, linker.",
    "Summary.scannow": "Check the app and its files.",
    "Help.scannow": "Check that each area is whole and print what is wrong; nothing is changed. app is this build against its source and its language and theme files, source the repository's files against this version, linker the installed mod, maps the map copies against the song list, edits every edited song, and data the settings and the files that keep the song list. Name several with commas, as in scannow maps, linker. What install -reinstall can put right is named at the end.",
    "Summary.song": "Show the loaded song.",
    "Help.song": "Show the loaded song's details, the same as info now.",
    "Summary.info": "Show a song's details.",
    "Help.info": "Show a song's id, title, artist, author, kind, length, notes (pressed ones), long notes, auto notes, difficulty, checkpoints, slot (origin for an original), hidden state and path.",
    "Summary.id": "Show song ids.",
    "Help.id": "Show song ids; an edit shows its slot. With nothing, the loaded song's.",
    "Summary.title": "Show song titles.",
    "Help.title": "Show song titles; an edit shows its slot. With nothing, the loaded song's.",
    "Summary.load": "Load a song.",
    "Help.load": "Load a base or custom song by any part of its title; a song with edits lists them to pick from, and a single match loads at once. * is every song and . is the loaded one, which is how it reloads or takes an option; link is the song the game is playing, its original unless -slot picks an edit, and needs the link on. An edit loads by its full title, as in 1*Plum - R. Songs hidden from the song list are left out unless -hidden asks for them. Unless keep speed on load is set, the playback speed goes back to 100%. -force loads an unsupported song anyway, after asking.",
    "Summary.playlist": "List songs.",
    "Help.playlist": "List songs with their id, kind and title. With nothing, every song; main is the main window's list in its own order, which is what this command used to print on its own.",
    "Summary.checkpoint": "Move to a checkpoint.",
    "Help.checkpoint": "Prepare the loaded song from one of its checkpoints, the way the main window's list does. A number is the checkpoint of that name, first is the song's beginning, last is the final one, and next and previous step through the list and wrap around at its ends. With nothing, it says where the song stands now, and list shows every checkpoint of the song.",
    "Summary.go": "Start from a tile.",
    "Help.go": "Prepare the loaded song from a tile number, as the section box does: play begins at the first note at or after that tile, with the lanes of a full run. The tile must be one this song presses, so a midspin tile or one inside a long note is refused. Tab offers the song's pins; checkpoint first goes back to the beginning.",
    "Summary.pin": "Pin a tile for go.",
    "Help.pin": "Pin a tile of a song so go can find it again: Tab after go offers the pins. A pin belongs to the song and every edit of it alike, whichever of them it was set on. now, the default, is the loaded song; all lists the song's pins in tile order, numbered as checkpoints are. The tile must be one go can start from.",
    "Summary.unpin": "Remove a pin.",
    "Help.unpin": "Remove a pin from a song and every edit of it. now, the default, is the loaded song, and Tab offers its pins.",
    "Summary.hide": "Hide a song from the song list.",
    "Help.hide": "Hide a song from the song list. show brings it back.",
    "Summary.show": "Show a hidden song again.",
    "Help.show": "Show a hidden song in the song list again.",
    "Summary.edit": "Edit a song's lanes and chords.",
    "Help.edit": "Open the editor for a song; with nothing, the loaded one. The settings close first; their unapplied changes are applied or dropped as you answer.",
    "Summary.note": "Show a song's notes.",
    "Help.note": "Show a song's notes as it plays, as n<note> <tile> <ms to next>; a chord shows as n3-4 3-4, and an edit shows its own lanes. now, the default, is what is on screen: while the editor is open, the lanes being edited, saved or not. Ranges: all, a..b, a.., ..b or n, where -1 is the last note. -json prints them as JSON instead, which override reads back.",
    "Summary.override": "Set the lanes from JSON.",
    "Help.override": "Replace the open editor's lanes with the JSON pasted after the command, as note -json prints it. Only the note numbers and tiles are read; a time that is there must match the note's own. The editor must be open.",
    "Summary.retitle": "Rename the loaded song.",
    "Help.retitle": "Give the loaded song the display name typed after the command, as the song list does. An edit keeps its n* prefix. Shows both names and asks first.",
    "Summary.sync": "Bring the map folder in.",
    "Help.sync": "Copy the map folder's songs into the custom maps and read every app folder again, as the main window's button does; it asks nothing. rebuild builds the custom maps from nothing instead, which is the only way a song no longer in the map folder leaves the list, and asks first.",
    "Summary.remove": "Remove an edited song.",
    "Help.remove": "Remove an edited song. restore brings back the one removed last.",
    "Summary.restore": "Bring back the removed song.",
    "Help.restore": "Bring back the edited song removed last. Only that one is kept, so a second restore finds nothing.",
    "Summary.delete": "Delete any song for good.",
    "Help.delete": "Delete a song for good; restore cannot bring it back. A custom map returns with the next sync.",
    "Summary.get": "Show a setting.",
    "Help.get": "Show a setting's value, as in get general.language.",
    "Summary.set": "Change a setting.",
    "Help.set": "Change a setting and apply it at once, as in set general.information-buttons false. A toggle also takes on/off, 1/0 and able/disable. rollback undoes it.",
    "Summary.speed": "Change the playback speed.",
    "Help.speed": "Change the speed the notes play at, the same as set timing.speed; with nothing, show it. -rate changes the approach rate instead, which is how long a note takes to come down (set display.travel, in ms, so a smaller number is faster). rollback undoes either.",
    "Summary.autosave": "Keep settings for the next run.",
    "Help.autosave": "Whether applying a setting also writes it to the store, the same as set general.autosave; with nothing, show it.",
    "Summary.property": "List sections, settings or values.",
    "Help.property": "With nothing, list the sections; with a section, its settings and values; with a setting, the values it takes.",
    "Summary.export": "Show the settings as JSON.",
    "Help.export": "Show the settings as JSON, as Export settings does. The map and data folders are left out.",
    "Summary.import": "Apply settings from JSON.",
    "Help.import": "Apply the settings named in JSON pasted after the command; the rest stay. Shows what changes and asks first.",
    "Summary.rollback": "Undo the last settings change.",
    "Help.rollback": "Undo the last change of the settings, whatever made it: the settings panel, a command, a load or a forced lane change. Each rollback steps back once more.",
    "Summary.reset": "Reset the settings.",
    "Help.reset": "Reset every setting to its default. The map and data folders stay, and rollback undoes it.",
    "Summary.do": "Run a settings action.",
    "Help.do": "Run an action of the settings panel.",
    "Summary.copy": "Copy what was printed.",
    "Help.copy": "Copy the last lines of the screen to the clipboard; with a number, that many messages back, * for all of them, -1 for the first, or a..b for a range. -commands keeps the typed commands with their Visualizer> prompt, -commands:only copies only those, -answers leaves out the answered questions, and -count skips the copying itself when counting.",
    "Summary.paste": "Show what was printed again.",
    "Help.paste": "Print the last messages of the screen again, counted the way copy counts them: a number goes that many back, * is all of them, -1 is the first and a..b is a range. -copy copies them as well. In a prompt slot, /paste puts them in.",
    "Summary.prompt": "Fill in a stored prompt.",
    "Help.prompt": "A prompt kept for pasting into a language model; with nothing, list the titles. A prompt with blanks asks for each one in turn: type the text, or /paste and /note -json to put an output in, /skip to leave the blank, /back /again /cancel to move around. The filled prompt is shown and copied once you answer Y. Nothing is sent anywhere and nothing about the app changes.",
    "Summary.report": "Write a note for the next version.",
    "Help.report": "A note left for whoever works on this next, kept in the app store until someone removes it. Every use says what it is doing, since a word is always needed here: create <title> starts one and opens it, and refuses a title that is already there unless -force replaces it after asking; write <title> opens one that is there to add to it, and refuses a title that is not. list shows them all with the version they were written for, and a word after it keeps only the notes holding it. print puts a note here instead of opening it; done and undone mark whether it is taken care of, a mark that lives beside the note where nobody can type it in or out; ask <title> > <why> says it is not settled and what is still needed, in the language the settings are set to; state prints that standing alone for an agent to read; erase removes a note and sweep removes every one already done; rename <old> > <new> gives one another name; colon lists the editor's : commands.",
    "Summary.update": "Get the latest version.",
    "Help.update": "Ask the repository whether this copy is the latest one. When it is, it says so; when a newer version is there, it asks, then closes the app, builds that version and opens it again, and an installed linker in the game is updated with it. -force goes to a version even when this copy's own changes stand in the way, throwing them away, and -version:1.0.0 picks the version; it always asks, and the risk is yours. The songs, the settings and the notes are never touched. It runs the repository's own update.ps1, so the terminal does the same thing.",
    "Summary.clear": "Clear the screen.",
    "Help.clear": "Clear the screen.",
    "Summary.exit": "Close the console.",
    "Help.exit": "Clear the screen and close the console. exit -keep keeps the screen for the next time, as closing the window does.",
    "Summary.date": "Show the date and time.",
    "Help.date": "Show this computer's date and time. Nothing is read from the game and nothing is saved.",
